⏰ CLAIM DEADLINE: {{ claim_deadline }}

Under California Revenue & Taxation Code § 4675, parties of interest
generally have ONE YEAR from the recording of the tax deed to file a claim
for excess proceeds. Missing this deadline may permanently bar your claim.

IMPORTANT: Verify the exact deadline with the county and, if possible,
with a licensed California attorney. The deadline stated above is an
estimate based on available records and may not be accurate.
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

STEP 1 — CONFIRM YOUR ELIGIBILITY

  [ ] Confirm you are a "party of interest" under RTC § 4675:
      — Former owner of record at time of tax sale, OR
      — Holder of a recorded lien, mortgage, or encumbrance, OR
      — Judgment creditor with a recorded abstract of judgment
  [ ] Confirm the deadline has not already passed
  [ ] Confirm the county has not already distributed the funds

STEP 2 — OBTAIN THE COUNTY CLAIM FORM

  [ ] Contact {{ county_agency }} to obtain the official claim form
      (Do not use a form from a prior year without verifying it is current)
  [ ] Confirm the current mailing address for submitting the claim
  [ ] Ask whether claims must be delivered in person, by certified mail,
      or electronically — and confirm accepted methods

STEP 3 — GATHER ALL REQUIRED DOCUMENTS

  Refer to the Document Request Checklist (separate document) for the
  full list. At minimum, you will typically need:

  [ ] Completed county claim form (Step 2)
  [ ] Valid government-issued photo ID
  [ ] Proof of your interest in the property (deed, deed of trust,
      judgment, or other recorded instrument)
  [ ] Notarized authorization if a representative is filing for you

STEP 4 — COMPLETE THE CLAIM FORM

  [ ] Fill in all required fields on the county form completely
  [ ] Use your full legal name exactly as it appears on the deed or
      other instrument supporting your claim
  [ ] Attach all required supporting documents
  [ ] Have your signature notarized if required by the county
      (VERIFY with {{ county_agency }})

STEP 5 — SUBMIT THE CLAIM

  [ ] Make complete copies of everything before submitting
  [ ] Submit by certified mail (return receipt requested) OR deliver
      in person and obtain a date-stamped receipt
  [ ] Record the date of submission and submission method
  [ ] Note any reference number or claim number provided by the county

STEP 6 — FOLLOW UP

  [ ] Follow up with {{ county_agency }} if no acknowledgment received
      within 30 days
  [ ] Keep all correspondence from the county
  [ ] If the county requests additional documentation, respond promptly

